
  riscvsim › demo 6 · Wide values · RV64
  RV64 registers hold 64 bits; see what addw keeps.

   1  # RV64 registers hold 64 bits; build 0x0000_0100_0000_0005
   2  addi x5, x0, 1     # x5 = 1
   3  slli x5, x5, 40    # x5 = 1 << 40
   4  addi x6, x5, 5     # x6 = 0x0000_0100_0000_0005
   5  addw x7, x6, x0    # addw keeps the low 32 bits: 5
   6  addi x10, x0, -1   # -1 sets all 64 bits

  cycle 1
    IF addi x5, x0, 1  ID ·  EX ·  MEM ·  WB ·
    › The first instruction, addi, is fetched from memory.

  cycle 2
    IF slli x5, x5, 40  ID addi x5, x0, 1  EX ·  MEM ·  WB ·
    › addi is decoded and reads its source registers.

  cycle 3
    IF addi x6, x5, 5  ID slli x5, x5, 40  EX addi x5, x0, 1  MEM ·  WB ·
    › addi x5 is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  cycle 4
    IF addw x7,x6,x0  ID addi x6,x5,5  EX slli x5,x5…  MEM addi x5,x0,1  WB ·
    › slli needs x5 from addi x5, so it is forwarded from the EX/MEM pipeline
      register and nobody waits.
      Forwarding: a result goes straight to the instruction that needs it,
      before it is written to its register.
      A pipeline register holds an instruction and its values between two
      stages.

  cycle 5
    IF addi x10…  ID addw x7,x6…  EX addi x6,x5…  MEM slli x5,x5…  WB addi x5…
    ✓ x5 = 1
    › addi x6 needs x5 from slli, so it is forwarded from the EX/MEM pipeline
      register and nobody waits.

  cycle 6
    IF ·  ID addi x10,x0…  EX addw x7,x6,x0  MEM addi x6,x5,5  WB slli x5,x5…
    ✓ x5 = 0x0000_0100_0000_0000
    › addw needs x6 from addi x6, so it is forwarded from the EX/MEM pipeline
      register and nobody waits.

  cycle 7
    IF ·  ID ·  EX addi x10, x0, -1  MEM addw x7, x6, x0  WB addi x6, x5, 5
    ✓ x6 = 0x0000_0100_0000_0005
    › addi finishes and writes x6 = 0x0000_0100_0000_0005.

  cycle 8
    IF ·  ID ·  EX ·  MEM addi x10, x0, -1  WB addw x7, x6, x0
    ✓ x7 = 5
    › addw finishes and writes x7 = 5.

  cycle 9
    IF ·  ID ·  EX ·  MEM ·  WB addi x10, x0, -1
    ✓ x10 = -1
    ✓ addi writes x10 = -1, and the program is finished.

  ✓ 9 cycles · 5 instructions · CPI 1.80 · 0 stalls · 0 mispredictions
    Ends with x5 = 0x0000_0100_0000_0000 · x6 = 0x0000_0100_0000_0005
              x7 = 5 · x10 = -1 (0xffff_ffff_ffff_ffff)
    No stalls: forwarding kept every instruction moving.

  ✓ You finished all six lessons.
  Next: riscvsim tui to step through your own program